{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Ethan jolted awake to the sound of a glitchy, distorted buzz filling the air. His bedroom walls flickered like an old television, pixels stretching and compressing as if reality itself were buffering. He rubbed his eyes and looked down—his fingers were slightly translucent, the edges shimmering with an unnatural sheen. The clock on his nightstand read 12:07 AM, but the sky outside his window was a gradient of neon blues and purples, with floating, blocky clouds that drifted lazily across the sky. When he tried to stand, his feet sank slightly into the floor, like he was stepping on a trampoline. A health bar appeared above his head, flickering between 100% and 99%, and in the bottom corner, the words **\"ERROR: PLAYER STATS LOADING…\"** flashed in jagged, red letters. He reached for his phone, but it was just a smooth, black slab—no buttons, no screen, just a single glowing word pressed into its surface: **\"CONTINUE?\"**\n\nEthan stumbled toward the mirror, only to find his reflection wavered like a corrupted file. His right eyebrow twitched—once, twice—before splitting into a jagged line of pixels that slowly reconnected. A voice, dry and synthetic, crackled from the speakers of his laptop, which had been sitting closed on his desk: *\"Welcome to *Ethan’s Dungeon*, version 1.0. Please choose your starting class: Warrior, Mage, or Rogue.\"* He turned to see his closet door creak open on its own, revealing a neatly arranged set of armor—a spiked pauldrons, a tattered cloak, and a sword hilt glowing faintly with runes. Outside, the street was now lined with NPCs milling about, their movements stiff and looped, as if they were caught in an endless replay. A child with oversized eyes waved at him and shouted, *\"New player spawn incoming!\"* before dissolving into static. Ethan’s hands began to tremble; if this was a game, then who—or what—was